What I accepted from the review, and why it matters:

The hook architecture correction was the important one. My P3 told the PreToolUse hook to fetch account state via the Binance read tools. A command hook talks over stdin/stdout only — it cannot initiate a tool call. That would have failed silently on Saturday night. The PostToolUse state observer is strictly better: Binance's own replies get overheard and hashed, the model never supplies the balance, and every observation is timestamped — which is what makes state.max_age_seconds enforceable at all.

That clause produces the nicest property in the build. Stale snapshot → escalate → the agent's natural fix is to check the balance → that read fires PostToolUse → snapshot refreshes → escalation clears. The control repairs its own precondition through the normal agent loop. No polling, no daemon, no special integration. Capture it cleanly in P3; it's the moment a technical reviewer decides this was designed rather than assembled.

I also cut both quotes that pitched against Binance, and the "every write requires confirmation" claim — TechCrunch is clear that autonomy is configurable, so we observe our connection and record what it actually does rather than assert either way.

